.TH GPT 1 "2025-10-10" "gpt-toolkit 1.4.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
gpt \- command-line interface for OpenAI GPT models
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gpt
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B gpt
is a Unix-style command-line interface for OpenAI's GPT models (GPT-4, GPT-5).
It reads prompts from stdin (or a file), sends them to the OpenAI API, and outputs responses to stdout.
Designed for pipe-based workflows and text filtering.
.PP
The tool follows Unix philosophy: text in, text out. It's composable, scriptable, and does one thing well.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS "Model Selection"
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-model " \fIMODEL\fR"
Specify the AI model to use (default: gpt-5)
.TP
.BR \-5
Use gpt-5 model (default)
.TP
.BR \-4
Use gpt-4 model
.TP
.BR \-\-mini
Use gpt-5-mini model (faster, cheaper)
.TP
.BR \-\-nano
Use gpt-5-nano model (fastest, cheapest)
.SS "GPT-5 Specific Options"
.TP
.BR \-\-reasoning\-effort " \fILEVEL\fR"
Control reasoning effort for GPT-5 models. Options: minimal, low, medium (default), high.
Higher values maximize quality but use more tokens. Lower values maximize speed.
.TP
.BR \-\-verbosity " \fILEVEL\fR"
Control output length. Options: low, medium (default), high.
.TP
.BR \-\-max\-completion\-tokens " \fIN\fR"
Maximum number of tokens to generate
.SS "Sampling Parameters"
.TP
.BR \-\-temperature " \fIFLOAT\fR"
Sampling temperature between 0 and 2 (default: 1.0). Lower values make output more deterministic.
.TP
.BR \-\-top\-p " \fIFLOAT\fR"
Nucleus sampling parameter (default: 1.0)
.TP
.BR \-\-stop " \fITEXT\fR"
Up to 4 sequences where the API stops generating tokens
.SS "Input/Output Modes"
.TP
.BR \-f " \fIFILE\fR"
Read prompt from FILE instead of stdin
.TP
.BR \-\-repl
Enable REPL mode for interactive conversations. Type 'exit' or 'quit' to exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show help message and exit
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B OPENAI_API_KEY
OpenAI API key (required)
.TP
.B OPENAI_ORGANIZATION
OpenAI organization ID (optional)
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS "Basic Usage"
.PP
Simple prompt:
.nf
.RS
echo "Count from 1 to 10" | gpt
.RE
.fi
.PP
Use GPT-4:
.nf
.RS
echo "What is 2+2?" | gpt -4
.RE
.fi
.PP
Read from file:
.nf
.RS
gpt -f prompt.txt
.RE
.fi
.SS "GPT-5 Reasoning Control"
.PP
Fast response for simple task:
.nf
.RS
echo "What is the capital of France?" | gpt --reasoning-effort minimal
.RE
.fi
.PP
Deep reasoning for complex problem:
.nf
.RS
echo "Solve this complex math problem..." | gpt --reasoning-effort high
.RE
.fi
.PP
Concise output:
.nf
.RS
echo "Explain quantum computing" | gpt --verbosity low
.RE
.fi
.SS "Interactive Mode"
.PP
Start REPL:
.nf
.RS
gpt --repl
.RE
.fi
.SS "Pipeline Examples"
.PP
Combine prompt with file content:
.nf
.RS
(echo "Summarize this:"; cat document.txt) | gpt
.RE
.fi
.PP
Extract and save code:
.nf
.RS
echo "Write a Python hello world" | gpt | gpt-extract-code > hello.py
.RE
.fi
.PP
Process multiple files in parallel:
.nf
.RS
for f in *.txt; do
  (echo "Improve:"; cat "$f") | gpt > "$f.new" &
done
wait
.RE
.fi
.SS "Shebang Usage"
.PP
Create executable GPT script:
.nf
.RS
#!/usr/bin/env -S gpt -m gpt-5 -f
Write a bash function that says hello
.RE
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/
Configuration files may be stored here (if implemented)
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success
.TP
.B 1
Error (API error, invalid arguments, etc.)
.SH NOTES
.PP
GPT-5 reasoning tokens count toward output token usage and billing.
.PP
The API is non-deterministic by default. Use
.B --temperature 0
for mostly deterministic outputs.
.PP
This tool requires an active internet connection and valid OpenAI API credentials.
